---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import InShort from '../../../components/article/InShort.astro';
import ZenTip from '../../../components/article/ZenTip.astro';
import ZenPicture from '../../../components/article/ZenPicture.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE } from '../../../consts';

const UPDATED = '2026-11-12';

const symptoms = await getCollection('symptoms');

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
    name: 'Symptoms of high cortisol',
    about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Hypercortisolism' },
    audience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
    lastReviewed: UPDATED,
    mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}/cortisol/symptoms/`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    name: 'Symptoms of high cortisol',
    url: `${SITE.url}/cortisol/symptoms/`,
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'ItemList',
      itemListElement: symptoms.map((s, i) => ({
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: i + 1,
        url: `${SITE.url}/cortisol/symptoms/${s.id}/`,
        name: s.data.title,
      })),
    },
  },
];

const proseCard = `app-card px-5 py-6 sm:px-8 sm:py-8 prose prose-base sm:prose-lg max-w-none
  prose-headings:font-extrabold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-headings:text-[color:var(--color-ink)]
  prose-headings:text-balance prose-headings:scroll-mt-24
  prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h2:mb-3 prose-h2:text-[1.5rem] sm:prose-h2:text-[1.75rem] prose-h2:leading-tight
  prose-p:text-[color:var(--color-ink)] prose-p:leading-[1.75] prose-p:my-4
  prose-li:text-[color:var(--color-ink)] prose-li:leading-[1.7] prose-li:my-1.5
  prose-a:text-[color:var(--color-teal-dark)] prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
  prose-strong:text-[color:var(--color-ink)]`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Signs of high cortisol — full symptom index"
  description="High and low cortisol symptoms explained — cortisol face, belly fat, 3am waking, fatigue — how symptoms cluster, and when it's time to see a doctor."
  image="/screenshots/wearable-insights.png"
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-6 pb-16 lg:max-w-6xl lg:px-8 lg:pt-10 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_22rem] lg:grid-rows-[auto_auto_auto_1fr_auto_auto] lg:gap-x-12">
    <header class="px-1 mb-6 lg:col-span-2 lg:row-start-1 lg:mb-4 lg:max-w-3xl">
      <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[color:var(--color-coral-dark)] font-semibold mb-2">Symptoms</div>
      <h1 class="text-[2rem] md:text-5xl leading-[1.1] font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3 text-balance">Symptoms of high cortisol</h1>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] md:text-xl leading-relaxed text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] text-pretty">
        The signs people link with high cortisol, which ones doctors actually look for, and which ones social media blows up.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] mt-4">
        Updated {new Date(UPDATED).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' })} · By <a href="/about/" class="underline underline-offset-2">Cortisol+ Editorial</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="lg:col-start-2 lg:row-start-2 lg:self-start"><InShort image="/app-ui/zen/zen-thinking.webp" points={[
      'Most "cortisol symptoms" also have <strong>many everyday causes</strong>.',
      'True high cortisol (Cushing\'s syndrome) is <strong>rare</strong> and diagnosed by a doctor.',
      '<strong>Symptoms alone can\'t diagnose</strong> a cortisol problem. Neither can a wearable.',
    ]} /></div>

    <div class={`${proseCard} lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-2 lg:row-span-3 lg:mt-6 lg:self-start`}>
      <p><strong>Cortisol is your body's main stress hormone.</strong> In short bursts it helps: it gets energy ready, sharpens focus and calms inflammation.</p>
      <p>Problems can start when cortisol stays high for weeks or months. Cortisol acts on many tissues, so the possible symptoms are wide-ranging. They're also easy to mix up with other conditions.</p>
      <p>Signs people often link with high cortisol include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>trouble falling asleep even when exhausted</li>
        <li>waking in the early hours</li>
        <li>weight that settles around the middle</li>
        <li>a "wired but tired" feeling</li>
        <li>mood swings and irritability</li>
        <li>slower recovery from workouts or illness</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Skin changes (acne, a rounder face), thinning hair and gut problems like bloating come up too. <strong>Every one of these has other common causes.</strong></p>

      <ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen/zen-journal.webp">
        Jot down <strong>when</strong> a symptom shows up and what else was going on. A simple note helps a lot when you talk to a doctor.
      </ZenTip>

      <h2>High vs. low cortisol: two different patterns</h2>
      <p><strong>Not every cortisol problem means <em>too much</em> cortisol.</strong></p>
      <p>Signs linked with high cortisol tend to feel "wired": trouble winding down at night, anxiety and racing thoughts, weight gain around the middle, a higher resting heart rate and blood-sugar swings.</p>
      <p>Low cortisol (adrenal insufficiency, such as Addison's disease) is a separate medical condition. Its signs tend to feel "flat": ongoing tiredness, low blood pressure or dizziness when you stand up, salt cravings and less energy for exercise.</p>
      <p>You may also see "adrenal fatigue" online. It is not a recognised medical diagnosis, according to the Endocrine Society. If you feel this way, a doctor can look for the real cause.</p>

      <h2>Clinical signs vs. TikTok trends</h2>
      <p><strong>True high cortisol from a medical cause (Cushing's syndrome) is rare.</strong> Doctors look for specific signs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>wide purple stretch marks</li>
        <li>a fatty hump between the shoulders</li>
        <li>bruising easily</li>
        <li>muscle weakness</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most people searching "cortisol face" or "cortisol belly" don't have Cushing's. Their symptoms are real, but they usually come from everyday things like stress, sleep, diet, alcohol or another health condition.</p>
      <p>We say on each symptom page what the evidence actually supports, so you can tell the two apart.</p>

      <ZenPicture src="/app-ui/zen/zen-doctor.webp" caption="Only a doctor can work out whether cortisol is really behind a symptom." />

      <h2>When to see a doctor</h2>
      <p><strong>Symptoms alone can't diagnose a cortisol disorder.</strong></p>
      <p>See a clinician if signs last, get worse, or include any of the Cushing's signs above. A doctor can decide whether <a href="/cortisol/test/">cortisol testing</a> makes sense, and which test to use.</p>
      <p>Everything on this site is educational. It is not a substitute for medical advice.</p>

      <a href="/start/" data-start-cta class="not-prose my-8 flex items-center gap-4 rounded-2xl bg-[color:var(--color-ink)] p-5 text-white no-underline shadow-sm transition hover:brightness-110">
        <img src="/apple-touch-icon.png" alt="" width="48" height="48" class="h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-[13px]" />
        <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 leading-snug">
          <span class="block font-extrabold">Get your personal stress plan</span>
          <span class="block text-sm text-white/75">A one-minute check-in with Zen, then your plan in Cortisol+</span>
        </span>
        <span class="shrink-0 rounded-full bg-white px-4 py-2 text-sm font-extrabold text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Start ›</span>
      </a>

      <p>For day-to-day tracking between doctor visits, Cortisol+ gives a 0–100 wellness score from wearable signals like HRV, resting heart rate and sleep. <strong>It does not measure or estimate cortisol</strong> (<a href="/how-it-works/">see how it works</a>).</p>
      <p>Want to reflect on your habits first? Try the <a href="/tools/cortisol-calculator/">60-second cortisol quiz</a>. It's a habits check-in, not a hormone test.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="px-1 mt-12 mb-4 text-[1.5rem] md:text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight leading-tight lg:col-span-2 lg:row-start-5 lg:mt-16">Browse symptoms</h2>

    {symptoms.length === 0 ? (
      <p class="text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] lg:col-span-2 lg:row-start-6">Symptom pages coming soon.</p>
    ) : (
      <ul class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:col-span-2 lg:row-start-6 lg:grid-cols-3 lg:gap-4">
        {symptoms.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/cortisol/symptoms/${s.id}/`} class="block p-4 rounded-xl border border-[color:var(--color-border)] hover:border-[color:var(--color-teal)]/40 hover:bg-[color:var(--color-surface)] transition">
              <div class="font-medium">{s.data.title}</div>
              <div class="text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] mt-1">{s.data.description}</div>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )}

    <div class="lg:col-start-2 lg:row-start-3 lg:self-start lg:[&>aside]:mt-6"><RelatedLinks links={[
      { href: '/cortisol/symptoms/cortisol-face/', title: 'Cortisol face', hint: 'The most-searched symptom' },
      { href: '/cortisol/levels/', title: 'Normal cortisol levels', hint: 'Reference ranges' },
      { href: '/cortisol/lower/', title: 'How to lower cortisol', hint: 'Evidence and limits' },
    ]} /></div>

    <aside class="mt-6 app-card p-5 lg:col-start-2 lg:row-start-4 lg:self-start text-sm text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-[color:var(--color-ink)]">Not medical advice.</strong> This page is educational. Symptoms and wearable scores cannot diagnose a cortisol disorder. For diagnosis, testing, or treatment of any medical condition, consult a qualified healthcare provider.
    </aside>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
